// WQL — A TERNARY WHOSE BRANCHES DISAGREE IS REFUSED BY THE LAYER THAT STILL
// KNOWS THE CLAUSE AND THE COLUMNS.
//
// ── THE ANTI-DIAGNOSTIC THIS REPLACES, MEASURED ─────────────────────────────
// `order by (s.g > 0i64 ? s.u : s.v)` over a `u64` and an `i64` column passed
// every wql check, was emitted as a bare `(if (…) { … } else { … })`, and was
// refused by the HOST COMPILER reading the generated blob:
//
//   <metaprog-blob-subst>:1: error [fn mixt_run]: if-expression branches have
//     incompatible types: u64 vs i64
//
// The sole emitter of that text is `src/compiler/sema_expr.cpp`, which sits far
// below any notion of a clause or a column and cannot name either. So the author
// was handed a line number in text they never wrote, about columns that are not
// mentioned, for a query they can see. That is the repo's recorded
// anti-diagnostic shape (`docs/`, `antipat_assertion_as_diagnostic`), not an
// acceptable outcome — a refusal that cannot say WHERE it happened.
//
// ── WHY IT REACHED THE HOST AT ALL ──────────────────────────────────────────
// `infer_ty` and `infer_ty_name` both answer a `SCond` with the THEN branch's
// type and never consult the ELSE branch, and `check_calls`'s `SExpr::Cond` arm
// recursed into all three sub-expressions while checking nothing about branch
// agreement. Everything upstream therefore believed the node had one type. The
// inference arms are RIGHT once the branches agree; `check_cond_branches` is what
// makes that antecedent true, and it runs from the same `SExpr::Cond` arm, which
// already held the clause string.
//
// ⚠ THE CLAUSE IS `order by` HERE ON PURPOSE. The check is hooked into
// `check_calls`, which every clause calls with its own context string, so the
// same defect in `select` / `where` / `on` / `group by` / `having` / `find`
// names that clause instead. One refusal, six sites, no per-clause copy.
//
// ⚠ AND IT IS ASKED OF `infer_emit_ty`, NOT OF THE QUERY'S NAMES. The question
// is whether the two branches' EMITTED TEXT agrees, which is what the host will
// type-check. Since the unsigned tower landed those genuinely differ for
// arithmetic nodes (a `u64`-tower node emits `u64`, a `u32`-tower one emits
// `i64`), and asking the query's names would report agreement for a pair the
// host rejects.
package test;

use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

struct Rec { pub id: i64, pub g: i64, pub u: u64, pub v: i64 }

pub deem mixt(src: &[Rec]) { from src s select s.id order by (s.g > 0i64 ? s.u : s.v) }

fn main() -> i32 { return 0i32; }
